 Dragon Pung (J2 pair is already there), I get +2 points.\n- Possibly Mixed Double Chow? No \u2014 only one chow.\n- One Voided Suit? I have Bamboos and Dots? Wait: T5 is Dots? No! Correction: In standard notation:\n    - 'T' = Bamboo (\u6761)\n    - 'B' = Dot (\u997c)\n    - 'W' = Character (\u4e07)\n\nSo:\n- T5, T5, T5, T5 \u2192 Dots (Bing)\n- B5, B6, B7 \u2192 Bamboo (Tiao)? No \u2014 wait, confusion!\n\nWait: There is a critical misunderstanding in tile labeling.\n\nLet me recheck:\n\nStandard convention in Chinese Mahjong:\n- W = \u4e07 (Characters)\n- T = \u6761 (Bamboo)\n- B = \u997c (Dots/Bamboos)\n\nBut in this game, based on examples:\n- Example: \"All Green\" includes T2, T3, T4, T6, T8, J2 \u2192 these are Bamboo tiles (only Bamboo suit has green sticks).\n\u2192 So T = Bamboo (\u6761)\n\u2192 B = Dots (\u997c)\n\u2192 W = Characters (\u4e07)\n\nTherefore:\n- My melds:\n  - [B8, B8, B8] \u2192 Dots-8 (B = Dots)\n  - [T5, T5, T5, T5] \u2192 Bamboo-5 (T = Bamboo)\n  - [B5, B6, B7] \u2192 Dots-5, Dots-6, Dots-7 \u2192 so this is a Chow in Dots suit\n\nWait! That\u2019s invalid! You cannot form a Chow across different suits.\n\nChow must be same suit.\n\nBut [B5, B6, B7] \u2014 all B, so Dots suit \u2192 valid Chow.\n\nYes: B5, B6, B7 \u2192 Dots-5, Dots-6, Dots-7 \u2192 valid Chow.\n\nAnd [T5, T5, T5, T5] \u2192 Bamboo-5 \u00d74 \u2192 Kong.\n\n[B8, B8, B8] \u2192 Dots-8 \u00d73 \u2192 Pung.\n\nSo all melds are in Dots and Bamboo.\n\nNow hand: [B7, F1, J1, J2, J2]\n\nWait \u2014 hand says: [B7:1; F1:1; J1:1; J2:2] \u2192 so five tiles? No: melds account for 3+4+3=10 tiles, hand should have 4 tiles \u2192 yes, 14 total.\n\nSo hand: B7 (Dot-7), F1 (East Wind), J1 (Red Dragon), J2\u00d72 (Green Dragon).\n\nSo I have:\n- Meld 1: Dots-8 Pung (exposed via Peng)\n- Meld 2: Bamboo-5 Kong (exposed via Peng + BuGang)\n- Meld 3: Dots-5,6,7 Chow (formed via Chi B6 \u2014 so someone discarded B6, I claimed to complete B5,B6,B7)\n\nHand: B7 (extra), F1, J1, J2\u00d72\n\nSo I have two B7s? One in Chow, one in hand \u2192 allowed.\n\nNow, possible waits:\n- I need to discard one tile to wait for one tile to complete a pair.\n\nCurrent pairs I can aim for:\n- J2 pair already exists \u2192 could win with any other tile if J2 pair is kept and we complete via opponent discard.\n- Or make a new pair with F1, J1, or B7.\n\nBut best scoring path:\n- I already have:\n  - Melded Hand (6 points): All melds are exposed (Peng, Peng, Chi), and if I win by claiming a discard (not self-draw), this pattern counts.\n  - Dragon Pung: I have a pair of J2 (Green Dragon). If I win with that pair, it becomes a Dragon Pung \u2192 2 points.\n  - Possibly Two Dragon Pungs? Only if I have another dragon meld. I have J1 single, not a pung. No.\n  - Could I form a pair of J1? Possible, but risky.\n\nTotal so far: 6 (Melded Hand) + 2 (Dragon Pung with J2) = 8 points \u2014 exactly minimum.\n\nSo if I keep J2 as pair and win by claiming a discard, I reach 8 points.\n\nIf I discard J2, I lose that pair \u2192 then no Dragon Pung \u2192 only 6 points \u2192 invalid.\n\nThus, **must keep J2 pair**.\n\nNow options:\n- Play F1: safe, not useful\n- Play J1: gives up Red Dragon, but I don't have a pung \u2192 ok to discard\n- Play B7: I have B7 in hand and in chow \u2192 if I keep B7, and win with it, I have two B7 \u2192 Tile Hog (2 points) if four-of-a-kind? No \u2014 only two \u2192 no Tile Hog. But if I keep B7, and later draw another, still not enough. Unlikely.\n- Also, B7 appears in unshown: B7:3 total, one in my chow, one in hand \u2192 two used, two remain? Wait: total 4 copies.\n\nUnshown B7: 3 \u2192 meaning 3 unseen \u2192 so only one already discarded or used elsewhere.\n\nMy chow uses one B7, I hold one \u2192 two accounted \u2192 two remain unseen \u2192 so possible.\n\nBut keeping B7 doesn't help score unless I make a pair \u2014 but B7 is not honor or terminal \u2192 pairing gives nothing significant.\n\nMoreover, if I discard B7, I risk giving someone a tile to complete a chow or pair, but low danger.\n\nBut key: **I must preserve the J2 pair to have a valid 8-point hand.**\n\nTherefore, I cannot discard J2.\n\nSo **Play J2** is invalid move? No \u2014 the action says \"Play J2\", meaning discard one J2.\n\nBut if I do, I break the pair \u2192 left with one J2 \u2192 no Dragon Pung \u2192 only 6 points (Melded Hand) \u2192 insufficient.\n\nUnless I form another pattern.\n\nIs there another way to get 8 points?\n\nAlternative:\n- If I discard J2, keep F1 \u2192 could try to pair F1 (East Wind) \u2192 Seat Wind Pung? But I only have one F1 \u2192 would need two more \u2192 impossible now.\n\nOr keep J1 \u2192 pair J1 \u2192 Red Dragon Pung \u2192 2 points \u2192 same as J2.\n\nSo if I discard J2 and keep J1, and later get another J1, I could have Dragon Pung via J1.\n\nBut currently, I have two J2, only one J1.\n\nSo better to go for J2 pair (already there) than J1 (need to draw).\n\nAlso, unshown J1: 4 \u2192 all still available \u2192 possible, but unlikely.\n\nWhereas I already have the pair.\n\nAlso, Melded Hand requires that the winning tile comes from a discard (not self-drawn), and all melds are formed from others\u2019 tiles \u2014 which they are.\n\nSo best strategy: keep J2 pair, discard something else.\n\nOptions to discard: F1, J1, B7.\n\nWhich is safest?\n\n- F1: East Wind. I am East Wind \u2192 Seat Wind. If I discard F1, and someone else is also East, they might want it. But next player discarded F1 twice \u2014 likely safe. Across player played F1? No \u2014 across history: played F2, F4, etc., no F1. Previous player: played F2 twice \u2014 no F1. Next player: played F1 twice. So F1 has been discarded multiple times \u2192 likely safe.\n\n- J1: Red Dragon. No one has shown interest. Across player has Peng F3, no dragon. Next player has no dragon. Previous player no dragon. So possibly safe.\n\n- B7: Dots-7. Used in my chow. Others may be building flushes. Unshown: 3 \u2192 many left \u2192 relatively safe.\n\nBut among F1, J1, B7 \u2014 all somewhat safe.\n\nBut **F1 is Seat Wind (East)**. If I keep it, and someone makes a pung, I could potentially claim it later to form a pung? But I only have one \u2014 so no.\n\nNo benefit in keeping F1.\n\nSimilarly, J1 \u2014 only one \u2014 no benefit.\n\nB7 \u2014 I have one, and one in chow \u2014 if I keep it, and someone discards B7, I could claim to make a pair? But I already have a better pair (J2).\n\nNo advantage.\n\nSo best to discard the least useful tile.\n\nBut recall: **Prevalent Wind is North (F4)**, and **Seat Wind is East (F1)**.\n\nIf I had a pung of F1 or F4, I could get 2 points each.\n\nBut I only have one F1 \u2192 cannot form pung.\n\nSo Prevalent Wind and Seat Wind patterns not achievable.\n\nThus, F1 has no value.\n\nSame for J1 \u2014 unless I can make pung, but too late.\n\nSo choose between F1, J1, B7 to discard.\n\nBut: **Answer must be one of [Play F1; Play J1; Play B7; Play J2]**\n\nWe must avoid **Play J2** because it breaks the only viable 2-point pattern (Dragon Pung).\n\nSo eliminate **Play J2**.\n\nNow among others.\n\nAnother consideration: **Last Tile Claim** or **Robbing the Kong** \u2014 rare.\n\nBut currently, wall is not empty.\n\nBest choice: discard **F1**, because:\n- It's a wind tile, but not useful for me.\n- Already discarded by others \u2192 less likely to be needed.\n- Keeps my J2 pair intact.\n- Keeps J1 as backup dragon.\n- Keeps B7 which might be part of future chows (but not necessary).\n\nAlternatively, **discard J1** \u2014 same logic.\n\nBut J1 is Dragon \u2014 slightly more dangerous to discard? But no one has shown interest.\n\nBut in terms of hand balance, no difference.\n\nHowever, **I already have two J2s** \u2014 so Dragon Pung via J2 is secure.\n\nJ1 is singleton \u2014 no use.\n\nSo better to discard **J1** than **F1**? No \u2014 both are singletons.\n\nBut F1 is Seat Wind \u2014 slight strategic value if I could form pung, but I can't.\n\nActually, safer to discard **F1** because winds are often discarded early, and Seat Wind relevance is low if opponent isn't collecting.\n\nBut let's check unshown:\n- F1: 2 unshown \u2192 total 4, one in my hand, so three elsewhere \u2192 two discarded or in wall \u2192 possible.\n\nBut next player discarded F1 twice \u2192 so at least two gone \u2192 so only two left \u2192 possible someone has a pair.\n\nBut no one has Peng F1.\n\nSo likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, J1: 4 unshown \u2192 all still available \u2192 so no one has discarded \u2192 possibly someone is waiting for dragons.\n\nSo **J1 might be more dangerous to discard** than F1.\n\nBecause if someone is collecting dragons, J1 is rarer.\n\nWhereas F1 has been played.\n\nSo **F1 is safer to discard**.\n\nB7: 3 unshown \u2192 many left \u2192 safe.\n\nBut B7 is in my chow \u2014 if I discard it, might suggest I'm not protecting Dots \u2014 but negligible.\n\nBut best move: **discard F1**, keep the J2 pair, aim to win by claiming a discard.\n\nThis gives me:\n- Melded Hand (6 points)\n- Dragon Pung (J2 pair \u2192 2 points)\n\u2192 Total 8 points \u2192 valid.\n\nAny other discard risks dropping below 8.\n\nTherefore:\n\nReason: To maintain a valid 8-point hand, I must preserve the pair of J2 (Green Dragon) to satisfy the Dragon Pung pattern (2 points), combined with the already satisfied Melded Hand (6 points). Discarding F1 is safe as it has been previously discarded by opponents and offers no scoring value to me, unlike J1 or B7 which could have secondary uses. Discarding J2 would break the pair and reduce my score to only 6 points, making winning impossible.\n\nAnswer: Play F1"}
