rently hold a pung of.]\\n\\nEvaluation: [My two valid actions are to discard either J1 or B7.  \\n- Discarding J1: This removes a potential Dragon pung (J1), but I don\\u2019t have a pair yet. J1 is safe to discard as no one has shown interest in red dragons; only one F1 remains unseen, and J1 appears unused. However, keeping J1 could help form a pair if I draw another later (though unlikely).  \\n- Discarding B7: I already have a chow including B7 (B5-B6-B7), and holding a single B7 risks forming a duplicate in a non-pair context. There\\u2019s only one B7 unshown (3 out of 4 already visible: one in my chow, one possibly discarded or held), so drawing another is improbable. Keeping it offers little value.\\n\\nThe hand currently has several scoring patterns:\\n- All Pungs (6 points): Already met with four pung/kong groups (T5 kong, J2 pung, B8 pung, and the B5-B6-B7 chow replaced conceptually \\u2014 wait, correction: I have **three pungs/kongs** and **one chow**, so not yet All Pungs.\\nWait: Melds are: [B8,B8,B8] (pung), [T5\\u00d74] (kong), [B5,B6,B7] (chow), [J2,J2,J2] (pung) \\u2192 that\\u2019s **three pungs/kongs + one chow**, so not All Pungs. To maximize score, shifting toward Full Flush (if all tiles are dots) or Half Flush may be viable. But I have T5 (bamboo), J2 (honor), B8, B5-B7 \\u2014 so mixed suits and honors.\\n\\nActually, current composition:\\n- Suits: Bamboo (T5), Dots (B5, B6, B7, B8), Characters absent\\n- Honors: J1, J2\\nSo two suits (dots and bamboo) plus honors \\u2192 not pure.\\n\\nBut: If I can win soon, possible patterns include:\\n- Mixed Triple Chow? No, only one chow.\\n- Outside Hand? Possibly: terminals/honors in every group. Let's check:\\n  - B8 pung: terminal (8 is not terminal; 1 or 9 are terminals) \\u2192 B8 is not terminal \\u2192 invalid.\\nOnly B8 is 8, not terminal. So no.\\n\\nBetter evaluation: Current hand structure is weak for high patterns. Best hope is:\\n- All Pungs (6 points) \\u2013 but I have a chow, not a 