laim Chicken Hand.\\n\\nTherefore, I must reach at least 8 points through combinations.\\n\\nCurrent fans:\\n- Dragon Pung (J2): 2\\n- Melded Kong (T5): 2\\n- One Voided Suit (missing W): 1\\nSubtotal: 5\\n\\nNeed 3 more points.\\n\\nPossibilities:\\n- Self Drawn: +1 (if I draw the winning tile)\\n- Single Wait: +1 (if only one tile completes the hand)\\n- Concealed Hand? No \\u2014 I have exposed melds \\u2192 fails\\n- Fully Concealed Hand? No \\u2014 exposed melds\\n- All Chows? Only one chow\\n- Mixed Double Chow? Only one chow\\n\\nNo path to 8 fans unless I get Single Wait + Self Drawn + something else.\\n\\nWait \\u2014 what about \\u201cPung of Terminals or Honors\\u201d? I have:\\n- B8 pung: 8 is not terminal \\u2192 not eligible\\n- T5 kong: 5 is simple \\u2192 no\\n- J2 pung: honor \\u2192 yes \\u2192 counts as Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point)\\nAlready counted Dragon Pung (2), but this is separate.\\n\\nSo add Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point) \\u2192 now 6 points.\\n\\nStill need 2 more.\\n\\nIf I win by self-drawn and single wait: +1 +1 = 8.\\n\\nYes! So total:\\n- Dragon Pung: 2\\n- Melded Kong: 2\\n- One Voided Suit: 1\\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors: 1\\n- Single Wait: 1\\n- Self Drawn: 1\\nTotal: 8 \\u2192 valid win\\n\\nTherefore, I must preserve the opportunity to win on a single tile by self-draw.\\n\\nNow, back to decision: discard J1 or B7?\\n\\n- If I discard J1, I keep B7. Then I can win only by drawing B7 (to make pair). B7: 3 already seen (one in my chow, one in discard history? Let's check visibility.\\n\\nUnshown B7: 3 \\u2192 means 1 is already revealed? Total 4 copies.\\n\\nWhere are they?\\n- My chow has one B7\\n- Unshown: 3 \\u2192 so only one revealed? But unshown means remaining in wall or in hands, not yet revealed.\\n\\n\\\"Unshown tiles\\\" likely means number not yet revealed to table (not discarded or in melds).\\n\\nStandard: 4 copies each.\\n\\nIn my meld: [B5,B6,B7] \\u2192 one B7 used\\nUnshown B7: 3 \\u2192 so total accounted: 1 + 3 = 4 \\u2192 so no other B7 has been discarded or used \\u2192 correct.\\n\\nSimilarly, J1: unshown 4 \\u2192 all still in play.\\n\\nSo B7: three copies still in play (could be in wall or opponents' hands)\\nJ1: four copies available\\n\\nThus, higher chance to draw J1 than B7? No: J1 has 4 unseen, B7 has 3 unseen \\u2192 similar.\\n\\nBut better to keep the tile with more copies? No: we want to discard the one less likely to be drawn.\\n\\nBut both are possible.\\n\\nHowever, discarding J1 removes a potential pair candidate, but keeps B7.\\n\\nBut if I discard B7, I keep J1 \\u2014 and J1 is a dragon, so pairing it gives no additional fan beyond what we already count (we already have Dragon Pung from J2, but J1 pair is not a pung).\\n\\nPairing J1 doesn't give Dragon Pung again \\u2014 only one pung per dragon.\\n\\nBut having a pair of J1 is just a pair.\\n\\nSame as B7 pair.\\n\\nBut strategically:\\n- Opponents may be wary of J1? No one has shown interest in dragons except player across punged F3 and J2 twice, but J2 is green dragon.\\n\\nJ2 has been discarded by multiple players (history shows Play J2 by across and previous), and I punged it, so J2 is dead.\\n\\nJ1: no one has discarded or claimed \\u2014 safer to keep?\\n\\nBut I need to discard one.\\n\\nKey insight: I should keep the tile that helps maintain flexibility and higher chance of win.\\n\\nBut both are equal in value.\\n\\nHowever, look at the chow: I have B5-B6-B7. Holding B7 is part of a completed chow, but I already used it. Having an extra B7 doesn't help unless I make a pair.\\n\\nBut B7 is 7 \\u2014 not terminal, not special.\\n\\nJ1 is a dragon \\u2014 more valuable in general.\\n\\nAlso, if I keep J1, and draw another, I can win with pair of J1.\\n\\nSame with B7.\\n\\nBut consider safety: who might be waiting?\\n\\nNext player has a chow in W4-W5-W6 \\u2014 possibly building Character-based hand.\\n\\nPlayer across has F3 pung \\u2014 North wind? F3 is West Wind.\\n\\nPrevailing wind is North (F4), my seat is East (F1).\\n\\nNo one has F4 or F1 pung.\\n\\nJ1 is Red Dragon \\u2014 not tied to wind.\\n\\nBut since no one has shown J1, it's safer to discard B7.\\n\\nMoreover, B7: is it useful to others? The chow B5-B6-B7 is already used by me. Others might be collecting dots.\\n\\nBut unshown B8: 1 left, B7: 3 left \\u2014 so B7 is abundant.\\n\\nBut I have only one move: discard one tile.\\n\\nNow, critical: after discard, I will draw, and perhaps win.\\n\\nBut I must choose which tile to discard to maximize win chance and safety.\\n\\nBut both discards leave me with one tile, needing to draw its duplicate.\\n\\nProbability of drawing J1: 4 copies unseen, total unseen tiles unknown, but roughly proportional.\\n\\nBut J1 has 4 unseen, B7 has 3 \\u2014 so slightly better chance to draw J1.\\n\\nThus, better to discard B7 and keep J1, hoping to draw J1.\\n\\nAdditionally, J1 is a dragon \\u2014 more likely to be useful in future if I survive, but no.\\n\\nAlso, in terms of pattern: if I pair J1, it's a dragon pair \\u2014 but no fan for that.\\n\\nBut no difference.\\n\\nAnother angle: the meld [B5,B6,B7] \\u2014 if I hold B7, and someone discards B4 or B8, I might think about extending, but I can't chi anymore because I already formed the meld.\\n\\nOnce a meld is exposed, I can't use those tiles again.\\n\\nSo holding B7 is just for pairing.\\n\\nSame for J1.\\n\\nBut note: if I discard B7, I break no structure.\\n\\nIf I discard J1, same.\\n\\nBut let's see the valid actions: [Play J1; Play B7] \\u2014 so I must choose one to discard.\\n\\nNow, consider defense: who might win?\\n\\nPrevious player just pung T4 \\u2014 building something.\\n\\nNext player has W4-W5-W6 chow \\u2014 possibly Half Flush or Pure Straight.\\n\\nBut I have no indication.\\n\\nSince I need to complete my hand, and I'm close, best to keep the tile with more copies unseen.\\n\\nJ1: 4 unseen, B7: 3 unseen \\u2192 so J1 is more plentiful \\u2192 higher chance to draw a J1 than a B7.\\n\\nTherefore, I should discard B7 and keep J1.\\n\\nThen, when I draw, if I get J1, I can win with pair of J1.\\n\\nFans:\\n- Win by self-draw \\u2192 Self Drawn (1)\\n- Only one tile completes (J1) \\u2192 Single Wait (1)\\n- Existing: Dragon Pung (2), Melded Kong (2), One Voided Suit (1), Pung of Terminals or Honors (1) \\u2192 total 8\\n\\nPerfect.\\n\\nIf I discard J1, and keep B7, then I need to draw B7 \\u2014 only 3 copies, slightly lower chance.\\n\\nSo better odds with J1.\\n\\nMoreover, J1 is honor, often safer to keep.\\n\\nReason: Higher availability of J1 (4 unseen vs 3 for B7) increases the probability of drawing a winning tile if I keep J1. Additionally, discarding B7 does not expose risk, as B7 is not a wind/dragon and not involved in current opponent melds.\\n\\nAnswer: Play B7\"}"}
